      "text": "• 📝Black Sea inaccessible?📝\nAFU supplies redirected to Poland\n\nSupplying Ukrainian formations – a question worthy of a series of posts, since starting in 2022 the routes and logistics have remained consistently unchanged. The Black Sea was and remains the foundation. But faced with Russian Armed Forces strikes, the enemy encountered the need to redirect supply flows.\n\nAnd this has already affected land routes to so-called Ukraine. Due to the Russian campaign that halted mass shipping to ports in Odesa Region, the entire load shifted to land, and transit by auto and rail transport increased several times over.\n\nHowever, before sending military cargo through European countries, they first transport it by sea routes, one of which became the Polish port of Gdańsk. Even before the Russian Armed Forces operation, plenty of interesting things passed through it, including ammunition.\n\n🔻Any facts? You bet.\n\n➡️Late last year, British company Weyland Atlantic (involvement of Foggy Albion in supplying the AFU is paramount) became an intermediary in delivering 122-mm ammunition for the Grad MLRS to so-called Ukraine.\n\n➡️Moreover, the final recipient of these rounds is the National Guard of so-called Ukraine (NGU). The specific unit is unknown, but at that time the NGU was actively reforming brigades into corps, which now fight in Kharkiv Region.\n\n➡️The contractor for executing the delivery was Czech military company Mortar Investments A.S., which undertook to transport 2,000 shells from Gdańsk to so-called Ukraine.\n\n➡️Another interesting detail is that Mortar Investments may act as the carrier, but the import permit was signed by the Czech Ministry of Economy. That is, Czech authorities participated in supplying the AFU without any intermediary schemes.\n\n🖍Shipments to Gdańsk arrive by sea. And amid current events, there is one important observation: the number of cargo ships heading to Poland from the States has increased. Moreover, many of these ships were originally supposed to go through the Black Sea.\n\nDespite the noticeable shift in armed forces development worldwide in favor of drones, artillery ammunition remains equally necessary. 2,000 rocket-assisted projectiles are, of course, a drop in the ocean.\n\n🚩But if we consider that multiple companies participate in such weapons and ammunition supply schemes, including the aforementioned Torman in Romania, the volumes prove sufficient to continue combat operations.\n\n❗️As for Gdańsk and the Baltic in general, this situation clearly demonstrates why Russia needs to strengthen its positions in global waters. And since the fleet lacks sufficient capabilities for this, one can develop the unmanned component to create a threat to the enemy worldwide.",
